1. China's makers went abroad when home sales collapsed, and took share from Japan first

Indonesia mined a record 836 million tonnes of coal in 2024 and 817 million in 2025. Its heavy-equipment market grew from about 18,200 units in 2023 to about 22,600 in 2025. Over the same two years Komatsu, the long-time market leader, sold about 750 fewer machines, and its share fell from 29% to 20%. Most of what it lost went to Chinese brands.

The shift reached most products and countries

Indonesia is not an isolated case. We tracked imports of seven kinds of equipment into India, Indonesia, Thailand and Malaysia. Between 2019 and 2024 China's share rose in 26 of these 28 product markets, and by 2024 it was half or more in 14. China supplied 74% of Indonesia's excavator imports in 2024, up from 33%, and 93% of India's imports of self-propelled cranes, up from 35%. Its share of electric-forklift imports was between 60% and 69% in all four countries.

Japanese brands lost the most

Most of China's gain came out of Japan's share. Across India, Indonesia, Thailand, Vietnam and Malaysia, Japan's share of imports fell between 2019 and 2024 in excavators, forklifts, moulding machines, machine tools, compressors, pumps and robots. The falls were steepest where Japan had been strongest. Its share fell from 46% to 26% in CNC lathes and from 31% to 19% in moulding machines, and in Thailand from 38% to 17% in forklifts. In Indonesia, Japan's share of excavator imports dropped from 24% to 7%. Hitachi's share of Indonesian sales of excavators above six tonnes fell from 21% in 2023 to 17% in 2025, alongside Komatsu's fall.

Others lost too. Korean and German makers held 9% and 7% of the region's imports of large generator sets in 2019, and 2% each by 2024. Taiwan and Korea lost ground in moulding machines, and Germany in laser machines. In India, the domestic crane maker lost most of its market (Chapter 3).

It began with a collapse at home

Chinese makers built their factories for a boom at home. Excavator sales in China peaked at 292,864 in 2020, and exports had been only 7 to 11% of units sold in the years up to then. Home sales then fell 69% in three years, to 89,980 in 2023. With plants to fill, the makers turned abroad, and in 2023 they exported more excavators (105,038) than they sold at home.

SANY shows the turn in one company. Its overseas revenue went from 23% of the total in 2021 to 45.7% in 2022, and to nearly two-thirds by 2025. Falling sales at home explain part of that rise, but overseas revenue also grew 47% in 2022 alone.

They stayed because selling abroad paid better

Had exporting been only a way to clear surplus machines, it would have faded once China recovered. It did not. In 2025 home sales rose 17.9% and exports rose 16.1%, to 116,739 units. Exports grew another 33.5% in the first half of 2026, with a record month in June.

The margins explain why. In 2022 SANY earned a gross margin of 26.4% abroad against 21.9% at home. By 2024 the gap had widened to 29.7% abroad against about 21% at home. XCMG earned 22.3% abroad and 19.3% at home in 2022, and now aims to make about 60% of its sales overseas by 2030. Robot makers are following the same path. A price war in China halved the price of some small robots, and in 2025 China exported more industrial robots than it imported for the first time.

Other explanations do not fit the evidence

A mining boom would lift every seller, yet Komatsu sold fewer machines in a growing market. Thailand had no coal or nickel boom, and China's share of its imports still rose in all seven products, from 36% to 69% in electric forklifts and from 24% to 53% in moulding machines.

US tariffs did not push Chinese machines into Asia either. The United States took 7.7% of China's construction-machinery exports in 2024, down from 11.6% in 2020, so the growth came from other markets.

The price gap was small. India's trade regulator found Chinese cranes only 0 to 10% cheaper than Indian ones. On Indonesia's public procurement catalogue, a 20-tonne SANY excavator is listed about 7% below Komatsu's equivalent. Exchange rates do little to explain the shift either, because Komatsu builds that excavator in Indonesia (Chapter 5).

Quality has caught up enough for everyday work. Indonesia's mainstream lenders now finance Chinese machines. Adira's lending to them doubled in 2024 and Mandiri Tunas Finance's rose 64%, with acceptable quality among the reasons Mandiri gave. Mining and other demanding work are a different matter, which Chapter 6 takes up.

The collapse at home explains why Chinese makers came. It does not explain why buyers and dealers switched to brands they barely knew when the price gap was so small. Chapter 2 follows one company, SANY in Indonesia, to show how that happened.


