Removed from the text earlier: "SANY, China's largest excavator maker" (no industry ranking found) and the yen line (no citation needed now).2. How SANY won Indonesia

SANY entered Indonesia in 2008. By 2025 it had delivered more than 43,000 machines there, and about 26,000 of its excavators were at work. Its distributor sold 10,299 excavators between 2015 and March 2022, and says SANY has been the country's best-selling excavator brand since 2021. Over the same years Komatsu's share of all heavy-equipment sales fell in two waves. It dropped from 29% in 2020 to 21% in 2021, recovered to 28 to 29% in 2022 and 2023, then fell to 26% in 2024, 20% in 2025 and 17% in the first four months of 2026.

SANY's gains came from four moves, made in order over more than a decade. Each made the next one easier, and price played only a small part.

The first buyers came from China

SANY's first large customers were Chinese-owned nickel projects. Chinese firms control about 75% of Indonesia's nickel refining capacity, and they bought from makers they already knew. Between 2014 and 2019 SANY supplied more than 560 machines, 455 of them excavators, to Tsingshan's nickel park at Morowali. That put SANY machines to work in the country before it had to persuade Indonesian buyers.

Indonesian buyers followed. By early 2022 North Sumatra, a province of plantations with no nickel mining, accounted for 30% of SANY's excavator sales. In 2024 the Jhonlin Group, an Indonesian conglomerate, signed for up to 2,000 SANY excavators for its agricultural projects.

A lender came years before the share gains

Most buyers borrow to pay for machines, and lenders were slow to back brands they did not know. As late as September 2024, the finance arm of state-owned Bank Negara Indonesia said it could not yet finance heavy equipment that was neither Japanese nor European. SANY's distributor worked around this by bringing its own partner. It signed with MNC Leasing in 2017, offered low-deposit loans in 2018 and launched a "buy without cash" scheme in 2019. That was two to four years before SANY's first large gain in share. By the time mainstream lenders began financing Chinese brands in volume (Chapter 1), SANY had a track record that buyers and lenders could see.

SANY sold into a segment Komatsu served thinly

Indonesia's nickel boom needed different machines from coal. In September 2021 a director of Astra, which owns Komatsu's distributor United Tractors, explained that nickel mines use excavators of 20 to 30 tonnes, while coal mines use machines of up to 300 tonnes. Komatsu had stayed focused on its coal customers. SANY sold into the gap, and in the first half of 2021 it held 26% of the market against Komatsu's 22%.

SANY also cut its prices by about 20% in 2021. That did not hold buyers on its own. When coal prices spiked in 2022, coal miners came back and Komatsu regained its share.

SANY had stock when buyers' margins collapsed

In 2022 demand ran at more than twice supply, and United Tractors moved Komatsu output from Japan and India to Indonesia to keep up. Then Indonesia's benchmark coal price fell from US$275.64 a tonne in May 2022 to US$125.85 in January 2024, even as output reached a record. Miners were digging more coal and earning about half as much for each tonne.

Komatsu's share started to fall in 2024, about 18 months after coal prices turned. Machines delivered in 2022 and 2023 had mostly been ordered at the peak. Orders placed in 2024 and 2025 came from buyers under pressure, and they chose machines that were available and a little cheaper. The lender BFI Finance said buyers picked Chinese brands because they were in stock, made in volume and increasingly accepted. SANY promoted its expanded plant at Karawang on shorter waiting times, and Chinese machines entered duty-free under the ASEAN-China trade agreement. In 2025 the market grew by about 5,600 units. Komatsu's sales grew by about 100.

United Tractors launched a cheaper second range for light and medium work, but Komatsu's share kept falling.

Then SANY built what keeps buyers

Volume paid for the assets that bring buyers back. SANY's distributor grew from 3 branches in 2015 to 26 in 2021, and in 2025 it opened a 1,500 square metre spare-parts warehouse in Jakarta. The Karawang plant now makes one excavator model with locally made parts. In September 2026 Indonesia's financial regulator licensed SANY's own finance company. Komatsu and Astra have run theirs since 2005.

What the case shows

The moves built on each other. Chinese-owned projects put SANY's machines to work, a partner lender made them affordable, the nickel segment brought volume, and stock was ready when coal buyers came under pressure. Moves made on their own achieved little. SANY's 20% price cut did not keep coal buyers in 2022, and United Tractors' cheaper range did not stop the slide in 2025. Chapters 3 to 5 take these moves in turn and test them in other products and countries.

