3. Win the buyers the leader serves thinly

Chinese makers did not attack established makers where they were strongest. They went after buyers the leaders served thinly. Then they removed the two things that stopped those buyers switching: waiting for a machine, and finding the money to pay for it. A small price gap did the rest.

New buyers often came from China

SANY's first Indonesian customers were Chinese nickel projects (Chapter 2), and the pattern has continued. In July 2026 XCMG opened its first overseas factory for electric machines in the Weda Bay nickel park, and its first loaders went to Tsingshan.

Factory equipment shows the same pull. Haitian, which makes plastic moulding machines, grew its overseas sales by 26.4% in 2025. It credited industrial customers building capacity abroad, and said demand for its main range was "driven by Chinese manufacturers accelerating their international expansion". Those manufacturers are arriving in numbers. Chinese firms made up 6% of the investors in Thailand's industrial estates in 2019 and 17% in early 2026, second only to Japanese firms. China's share of moulding-machine imports into India, Indonesia, Thailand and Malaysia rose from 24% in 2015 to 60% in 2025.

Or wanted machines the leader neglected

The other way in was a machine the leader did not push. In Indonesia it was the 20 to 30 tonne excavator that nickel mines use (Chapter 2). In India SANY started with long-reach excavators, a small segment, and by its own account took 52% of it within about 18 months in 2016. It then sold cranes and piling rigs through the same dealers.

Have the machine when the leader is rationing

Indonesia showed how much stock matters to buyers under pressure (Chapter 2). Generators for data centres show the same effect in another product. Data-centre building has outrun the established brands' capacity for large generator sets. In August 2026 Cummins said its growth was "constrained by available capacity for larger generator-set configurations", and that it was generally selling new power-generation equipment into the second half of 2028. It named Southeast Asia among the markets where demand for data-centre backup power was particularly strong.

Chinese makers filled the gap. China's share of Malaysia's imports of large generator sets rose from 20% in 2019 to 84% in 2024, as those imports grew from US$47m to US$326m. In 2025 they reached US$595m, and China still supplied 82%. In 2024 Weichai reported that its Baudouin generator sets were already in use at data-centre projects in Johor.

Take away the credit barrier

In India 85 to 90% of construction equipment is bought on credit, mostly from non-bank lenders, and first-time buyers pay higher rates. A brand that lenders do not know is hard to sell, however good the machine. By 2020 Chinese makers in Southeast Asia were offering buyers incentives such as zero-down-payment finance, according to a Nikkei report on Komatsu's response.

India's crane market gives the clearest proof that credit moves buyers. In 2025 India's trade regulator found that XCMG's Chinese exporter had given its related Indian importer up to three years' credit. Chinese cranes were only slightly cheaper than Indian ones (Chapter 1), yet Chinese imports rose from 163 units a year to 938, and the Indian producer's market-share index fell from 100 to 17. SANY India says the Jammu and Kashmir Bank finances about 80% of its machine sales in that region.

Where it stops

Where there was no gap, little moved. Thailand had no mining boom, and so no new group of excavator buyers. China's share of Thai excavator imports barely moved, from 35% in 2019 to 36% in 2024, while Japan's rose from 40% to 51%. Over the same years China's share of Thai forklift and moulding-machine imports roughly doubled, so Thai buyers were not avoiding Chinese goods. In India, JCB leads the backhoe-loader segment by a wide margin and Chinese brands have made little headway against it. In the year to March 2026, JCB registered 34,632 construction machines in India and LiuGong registered 1,061.

Credit also stopped at the factory gate. We found no Chinese maker offering finance to buyers of compressors, pumps, machine tools or robot arms in India or Southeast Asia.

For established makers, the warning signs are a group of buyers they barely serve, a size they do not stock, a delivery queue, and buyers who cannot borrow to buy their machines. Chapter 8 returns to each.

