4. Dealers follow volume: how Chinese makers won the channel

Most machines reach their buyers through someone else: a distributor, a dealer, a lender. Chinese makers won those intermediaries as well as the buyers. They offered what the established brands were not offering: growth at the cheaper end of the market, finance for the dealer's customers, stock and parts from nearby plants, and in some cases a share of the business. Some of the established makers' own dealers took them up on it.

Dealers changed sides

In 2020, in a weak Indonesian market, the listed distributor Intraco Penta ended its dealer agreements with Volvo Construction Equipment and took on LiuGong instead, through a partnership with LiuGong's Indonesian company. LiuGong reached about 4% of the national market within months. By 2024 Intraco was counting on the next stage of the deal: the LiuGong machines it had sold since 2020 were reaching the age for component replacement, which meant parts income. By late 2025 it was telling investors that cost-conscious customers could make lower-priced Chinese machines their main choice.

Kobexindo, another listed distributor whose brands include Doosan, has gone the same way. In August 2026 its director said the company's strategy centred on Chinese products, which "offer more competitive prices" and can reach a wider market. Its machine sales rose 47% in the first half of the year. Even United Tractors, Komatsu's own distributor, now sells a wide-body mining truck under the Komatsu name that International Mining understands is built by the Chinese maker Tonly (Chapter 7).

Five routes to the buyer

SANY has described its overseas model as varying by market: joint ventures with local partners in Indonesia and Thailand, its own subsidiary and plant in India, and established dealers in Europe and the United States. Across the products in this paper, Chinese makers used five routes.

The first is a dedicated distributor supplied by a local plant. In Indonesia, SANY's distributor had 44 branches by 2026, 14 of them in Kalimantan and 9 in Sulawesi, where the coal and nickel mines are. XCMG's dealer, GM Tractors, had 14 branches in 2023 and added service points in Kalimantan's mining areas.

The second combines independent dealers with outlets the maker runs itself. SANY India has about 42 dealers and more than 260 sales and service points. When it appointed Om Infracore as sole dealer for Chhattisgarh in 2023, 307 SANY machines were already at work in the state. In early 2026 it opened a dealer outlet in Indore and a company-run one in Thiruvarur within days of each other.

The third is an importer owned by the maker, as in XCMG's crane business in India (Chapter 3).

The fourth is a network that came with a brand the maker bought. XCMG sells its excavators and loaders in India through Schwing Stetter India, the Indian arm of Germany's Schwing, which XCMG owns and which has made concrete machinery in India since 1999.

The fifth is a partner. Heli is building its Thai forklift and battery plant in a joint venture with Siam Motors Parts. Siasun sells robots in Thailand through a joint venture with Somboon, an auto-parts group whose customers include Toyota and Honda. Chinese pump and compressor makers mostly sell through other firms' distributors: Leo's Indonesian business works through about 12.

What dealers and partners get

The first thing is growth, which Kobexindo's 47% shows. The second is finance for the dealer's customers. SANY's Indonesian distributor brought in MNC Leasing in 2017 (Chapter 2). LiuGong has worked with the bank UOB since 2018 on a finance framework for its buyers in Indonesia and Thailand; a LiuGong executive explained that "our customers told us that they require machinery financing assistance". In 2025 SANY signed customer-finance agreements worth about RMB1.45bn, one of them with a leasing company in the Tiara Marga Trakindo group, whose dealer Trakindo sells Caterpillar machines.

Some partners get a share of the lender itself. Shandong Heavy Industry, Sinotruk's parent, bought 40% of MNC Leasing in 2024, and in September 2026 XCMG and its dealer GM Tractors announced a finance company as a joint venture. Dealers also get stock and parts close at hand. SANY's distributor opened a 1,500 square metre parts warehouse in Jakarta in 2025, and SANY promotes its Karawang plant on shorter waiting times. And they get a service promise they can sell: XCMG's Indonesian policy commits to responding within 15 minutes, finishing a repair within 24 hours and delivering parts within 72.

Where it stops

Deep dealer networks still hold where loyalty is old. Tata Hitachi makes about 90% of its sales through some 230 dealer outlets, and it has won back part of its mining business (Chapter 6). A network that grows faster than its parts supply also leaves dealers exposed. When the Chinese carmaker Neta's parent went bankrupt in 2025, 18 of its Thai dealers sought more than THB200m, and some repairs waited up to ten months for parts. That case is a car maker's, but the lesson applies to any dealer taking on a new brand.

For established makers, dealers follow volume. A dealer that an established brand cannot help grow will find a brand that can. Chapter 8 returns to this.

