5. Build behind the wall

When governments put up barriers, Chinese makers built factories behind them. Duties cut Chinese imports where they were imposed, but they did not push Chinese makers out. The contest moved inside the border.

In India, the plant came first

India makes more than 95% of the construction equipment it buys, so a maker that wants volume there has to build there. SANY opened its plant at Chakan, near Pune, in 2009 and 2010, years before it led any segment, and expanded it in 2025 to more than 14,000 machines a year. XCMG, Zoomlion and LiuGong have since built or expanded Indian plants of their own. By SANY's own figures, its Indian revenue grew from about Rs970 crore in 2016 to between Rs8,000 and 9,000 crore in 2024, and it now has more than 35,000 machines at work in India.

Duties cut imports, then the makers moved in

India has put anti-dumping duties on Chinese factory equipment, and they did cut imports. In December 2023 it imposed duties of 22.5 to 147% on Chinese machines for laser cutting, marking and welding. India's imports of these machines from China fell from US$137m in 2023 to US$63m in 2024, and China's share from 66% to 47%. The duties also caught Western brands' Chinese factories, including Bystronic's. The leading Chinese brand stayed. HSG Laser says it still led India's laser-cutting sales in 2023 and 2024, and in 2025 it announced a US$10m factory in India.

Moulding machines followed the same course. After duties of up to 63% took effect in June 2025, China's share of India's moulding-machine imports fell from 53% to 47% in a year, while Japan's rose from 21% to 25%. Yizumi, one of the Chinese makers named in the case, already had an Indian plant, which had delivered 3,500 machines by September 2025. The firms that asked for these duties included Shibaura Machine India and Milacron India, established makers with Indian plants of their own.

Cranes went differently. India's trade regulator recommended duties on Chinese cranes in September 2025, but by August 2026 the government had still not imposed them.

In Indonesia, local content decides public purchases

Indonesia's rules work through government buying rather than duties. Public buyers must choose a product with more than 40% local content where one exists, and since December 2025 investing in Indonesia earns a maker extra local-content credit. On the government's procurement catalogue, Komatsu's 20-tonne excavator scores 51.2%. SANY's equivalent scores nothing, because it is listed as an import. Chinese makers are responding with plants. LiuGong broke ground on a US$317m factory at Karawang in July 2026, with a first phase of 5,000 machines a year, and committed to raising its local content.

India is steering the next wave in a similar way. Since May 2026 its Press Note 2 has offered a 60-day approval route for investment in capital goods from countries that share a land border with India, provided Indian residents keep majority ownership and control.

For established makers, local plants and local content are assets they already hold, and they win public tenders. They do not keep Chinese makers out for long. The contest that follows is fought on service and uptime, which Chapter 6 takes up.

